WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.970
Competition &amp; information

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:09.070
Why are cartels unstable?

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:14.910
Collusion is a prisoner’s dilemma — each firm’s private incentive is to cheat and undercut.

4
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:20.450
What is HHI and why use it over a firm count?

5
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:34.010
The sum of each firm’s squared percentage market share — four firms at 25% gives 2,500, a pure monopoly 10,000. It captures share; twenty firms where one holds 95% is not competitive.

6
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.950
Adverse selection vs. moral hazard?

7
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:48.450
Adverse selection hides a type, before the deal — only sick people buy the insurance. Moral hazard hides an action, after it — insured drivers drive recklessly.

8
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.430
Explain the death spiral.

9
00:00:52.430 --> 00:01:03.550
Insurers raise premiums to cover a sick pool, the healthiest drop out, the pool gets sicker, premiums rise again — adverse selection eating itself until the market can collapse.

10
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.950
Does every principal–agent relationship have moral hazard?

11
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:17.060
No. Only when the principal cannot observe what the agent actually does — exactly the kind of distinction he writes a multiple-choice question around.

12
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.000
Competition &amp; information — 5 cards
